Original repository path: docs/SHARED_SCHEDULER_VERIFICATION.md

# Shared processing scheduler: implementation and evidence

Recorded 2026-09-16; origin admission added 2026-09-17. This implements a
foundation of migration §8A. The full
[migration plan](../MIGRATION_PLAN.md) and [execution ledger](MIGRATION_STATUS.md)
remain authoritative; this document does not certify throughput or production
readiness.

## Implemented behavior

- Editor rendering, decoder preview proxies, interactive batches, and folder
  jobs use one app-owned scheduler. Logical UI clients do not own physical
  workers. Engines start lazily and remain reusable across jobs; idle workers
  retire after 30 seconds, and oversized idle heaps can retire sooner to admit
  smaller work. The published generated JS/WASM remain unchanged.
- CPU tokens and estimated transient/resident memory are shared across image
  and folder worker kinds. The policy includes encoded-buffer copies, live
  image planes, output estimates, retained source/result buffers and observed
  WASM heap sizes. It budgets today's full-size preview encoder honestly.
  Browser `deviceMemory` and logical CPU reports are hints; these estimates are
  not process RAM measurements or a guarantee against browser termination.
- Source copying occurs in a preparation callback after admission. Folder
  work first reads metadata, pins the attempt's size/timestamp, and inspects
  image dimensions under an encoded-byte budget. Render admission uses the
  actual dimensions; a second size/timestamp check catches a changed source.
  Failed-source Retry accepts a newly repaired source. Inspections read encoded
  input again during rendering; avoiding that repeated I/O is a future measured
  optimization. Header access was checked against Pillow's pinned source
  revision `310788f9fcadc85b02263b383c5a6ea094b000c6`: `Image::Bytes` stores
  metadata and `size()` reads it without materializing pixel payloads.
- Current-image work has priority; queue aging prevents background starvation.
  Admissions drain toward a waiting large task instead of allowing smaller
  arrivals to keep it waiting indefinitely. Cancellation removes queued intent
  or terminates an active worker, including synchronous WASM. Worker deaths,
  startup failures, and task timeouts release reservations and leave UI retry
  paths available. Wire revisions are monotonic across reused workers while
  preserving each client's original revision in delivered messages.
  If an asynchronous source preparation is still settling after cancellation,
  its memory credit remains reserved even though the worker/CPU token is freed.
  Closing a scheduler also waits for that preparation before releasing its
  origin reservation. Browser context destruction releases both through the
  browser lifecycle.
- Folder output bytes remain in their worker until `writable.close()` completes.
  The reservation therefore includes slow writes and prevents unbounded output
  read-ahead. Pause returns queued claims to pending while admitted images
  finish; paused work is not silently treated as completed.
- Auto starts at one runnable worker. Before the first useful result, a batch
  with at least four substantive queued/admitted tasks may grow an initial
  probe as real work arrives. The limit is bounded by two waves of demand,
  half the reported CPU budget (with a two-worker minimum) and eight workers;
  the existing CPU budget must be at least three and memory at least 1 GiB.
  Missing/small hints, short edits and header-only work retain the serial start.
  Normal memory/origin admission still applies, and pressure disables the
  initial probe. The [20 September policy and evidence](research/2026-09-20/auto-calibration/README.md)
  supersede the earlier [two-worker experiment](research/2026-09-17/collections/bounded-startup/README.md).
  It compares windows of comparable work,
  grows in steps, and retreats with a cooldown when a larger pool adds no
  completion-rate improvement. Max speed uses the reported CPU ceiling subject
  to memory admission. Low resource requests one worker. Existing admitted
  tasks can finish after lowering the limit. Long-task observations, worker
  failures and reported allocation errors reduce future admission. The tuning
  thresholds are provisional and require the plan's real-corpus benchmarks.
  The collection work on 17 September repaired class transitions that erased
  calibration: at most 16 class-specific windows now survive interleaved header
  reads and other image sizes. Header reads cannot calibrate render throughput,
  and a completion started under another limit is excluded from calibration.
  The existing eight-completion/250 ms, 5% gain and cooldown rules are preserved.
  Failed expansion also challenges an initial guess that has no measured lower
  baseline. A lower-count trial compares the same work family, keeps fewer
  workers when extra workers bring no qualifying gain, and restores the measured
  good count when the trial is slower or ends incomplete. Pressure discards its
  old baseline. The initial eight-worker bound does not cap later measured growth.
- Diagnostics retain at most 64 timing records with workload classes and
  aggregate resource counts. They include no filenames, job IDs, paths or image
  bytes and are not sent to a server.
  Startup timing also retains at most 64 records. Opt-in folder/render stage
  observations and a pinned, journaled collection matrix are documented in
  [Collection benchmarks](COLLECTION_BENCHMARKS.md). The materialization/encoding
  interval includes deferred Pillow transforms; it does not isolate the codec.

## Resource admission across tabs

Where Web Locks are available, cooperating editor tabs in the same browser
storage partition share CPU and estimated memory admission. Each tab publishes
one live reservation containing only random identity, resource counts and its
device budgets. An exclusive admission turn checks all reservations against the
smallest participating CPU/memory hints. It reserves a bounded group of eligible
tasks before starting engines or preparing source bytes. Grouping avoids an
origin-lock round trip for every tiny image. Tasks still use separate physical
workers and may run concurrently in different tabs.

The ledger consists of held Web Locks, with no expiring IndexedDB/localStorage
lease and no heartbeat that authorizes taking a live tab's capacity. Browser
context destruction releases the reservation. Replacement publishes the new
reservation before releasing the previous one; a reader that sees both counts
conservatively. FIFO admission turns drain toward a waiting request. Each tab's
foreground priority and queue aging still apply, and an unadmitted background
request yields when a foreground request arrives.

Active reservations include output writes, idle WASM heaps and the retained
surfaces currently reported by the app. A waiting tab asks peers to retire idle
workers. Observation updates can report more memory than an earlier estimate;
that debt blocks further admissions instead of being clamped or discarded.
BroadcastChannel only wakes waiters and requests idle retirement. Without it,
bounded polling rechecks the authoritative locks; it never steals a reservation.
Native lock errors fail processing rather than silently creating an independent
pool. Browsers without Web Locks retain the per-tab scheduler and expose `tab`
scope in local diagnostics. The UI identifies waits for capacity in another tab.

This coordinates the current app's participating contexts, not other browser
profiles, browsers, applications or older builds that do not participate.
Counts remain estimates, not measured process RAM. Complete surface/model/cache
accounting, physical process crashes, background/frozen-page behavior and safe
multi-version rollout remain release qualifications.

The design uses the browser's [Web Locks lifecycle and query
contract](https://www.w3.org/TR/web-locks/), not timing-based liveness inference.
See [implementation](../src/processing/origin-admission.js) and the
[real-browser fault checks](../tests/origin-admission.browser.mjs).

## Evidence obtained

`npm run verify:scheduler` passes 38 behavioral tests: combined admission,
deferred preparation, engine reuse, priority/aging, retained/output/idle memory,
15 simultaneous admitted tasks on a simulated 16-thread hint, cancellation,
stale-message rejection, worker death and timeout, reentrant completion, Auto
growth/retreat/cooldown, retirement of oversized idle heaps, retained memory
credit for a cancelled asynchronous source read, and synchronous shutdown during
a grouped worker-start failure, mixed-class calibration, header-only exclusion,
and calibration across idle gaps and success/error/cancellation drains.
Seven startup checks add the bounded two-worker probe, conservative resource
hints, short/header-only demand, memory/output reservations and pressure
suppression without weakening subsequent eight-sample growth/retreat checks.
Ten later checks cover incremental initial arrivals, closure after useful work,
cancelled startup, retained-memory/output credits, lower-count trials on modeled
saturation, unfinished-trial restoration, measured growth beyond eight and
isolation between work families.
A cancelled task cannot be dispatched later in
the same admitted group. Fake workers
are used only for controlled scheduler state/failure tests.

The full source Chromium regression suite passed after integration, including
editing, batch results, mobile sheets, export, folder pause/resume, repaired
source retry, and recovery. The packaged build also passed. Its real runtime
check rendered 24 small fixture images at each of 1, 2, 4 and 8 workers on this
host. Every output matched the one-worker PNG bytes; item coverage and revision
mapping were exact, with no observed admission violations. Counts 16 and above
were not run on this host because its reported CPU budget did not admit them.
These small-fixture checks are correctness tests, not performance measurements.

The origin-admission browser regression uses real Pillow workers in separate
pages of one browser context. It verifies that both tabs can run simultaneously
within a combined three-token budget, that queued sources remain unread, and
that cancelling a queued item produces no output. Surviving outputs match byte
for byte. Estimated-memory pressure blocks admission independently of CPU,
retires peer idle engines, preserves retained-source debt and keeps cancelled
read credits until preparation settles. Closing a context releases its active
claims and permits a waiting tab to continue. An additional page cannot even
start its engine while all CPU tokens are held. Foreground reprioritization,
operation without BroadcastChannel, fail-closed query errors and complete
reservation cleanup are covered. Artificial preparation gates and memory
estimates make these state transitions deterministic; they do not measure disk
speed, observed RAM, throughput or a native process crash.

The full source and packaged Chromium suites passed after grouped admission and
the asynchronous-read accounting fix, including the existing editor, story,
photo-look, folder, recovery and phone regressions. After a final dispatch guard
and more precise waiting-status wording, the focused origin-admission browser
suite passed again against both source and the rebuilt artifact. The guard also
has the synchronous-close regression above. At that earlier milestone,
`npm run verify` passed thirteen scheduler checks and 65 project/style/recovery
checks; the current total is 38 scheduler and 124 model/diagnostic checks.

`npm run verify:folder-recovery` also passes: real Web Locks/IndexedDB/OPFS,
24 unique parallel claims, ownership handoff, terminated writers, exactly-once
counters and conflict preservation. This confirms compatibility with existing
job ownership; it does not replace native filesystem/crash qualification.

That earlier artifact had 151 files: 410,429 bytes of app JavaScript
(131,017 bytes in Brotli sidecars), 60,189 bytes of CSS (9,510 Brotli), and the
unchanged 4,548,741-byte published WASM (1,170,855 Brotli). These packaging counts
are not measured load times or runtime memory. Documentation links and
`git diff --check` pass.

Exact legacy/target adapter comparisons still pass 33/33. The existing 8×8
latency benchmark's failed budget remains open; a green scheduler test does not
replace that result or prove the proposed desktop 2× throughput target.
The bounded-startup revision passes full source and packaged Chromium suites,
folder-recovery faults, 33/33 adapter comparisons and the 179-file artifact
check. Its completed canonical timing run passes all output checks and has
15 passing, 2 failing and 4 unproven budgets. Both camera blocks and warm small
images met timing; cold small Auto and tiny PNG failed at that revision. Prior matrices
describe their own archived source revisions.

The later [Auto startup calibration](research/2026-09-20/auto-calibration/README.md)
passes all 162 deterministic checks, the complete source and 206-file packaged
Chromium suites, recovery, parity and the declared coverage slice. Its completed
canonical matrix checks 100 measured jobs / 3,600 images and 174 total jobs /
6,264 outputs. All outputs pass, and all four Auto families meet the 10% target
against eligible fixed settings. The ledger is 16 pass, 1 fail and 4 unproven;
tiny PNG still fails and fixed 16 remains ineligible. Source/package inventories
and final raw/source/input checks passed. These desktop results do not establish
physical-phone, process-memory, sustained-thermal or heavy-scene qualification.

The subsequent [folder rendering contracts](FOLDER_RENDER_CONTRACT_VERIFICATION.md)
bring the deterministic total to 167 (38 scheduler, 129 model/diagnostic checks).
Font capture and verification occur only inside an admitted full-render task.
Folder text reserves eight times the bounded font bytes for reads, hashes,
storage copies and rasterization; that estimate is not process memory evidence.
The first snapshot is exclusive, while rendering and ordinary writes remain
concurrent. Exact-output checks observe peaks 1/4/8 with no admission violation.
These new validation steps change the source identity, so the earlier canonical
timing results do not qualify the current revision.

The [multiple-shape story export workflow](STORY_EXPORT_VERIFICATION.md) raises
the deterministic total to 175 (38 scheduler, 137 model/diagnostic checks).
It queues copied scene revisions through the same scheduler and adds completed
File sizes to the workspace's retained-byte ledger. Pause keeps successful files;
retry runs only failed outputs, and closing drops both URLs and file references
even if an aborted renderer settles later. Reduced-size paired-variant output
checks observe 1/4/8 workers with exact serial results, and the phone workflow
independently decodes eight full-size exports. These are correctness observations;
physical memory, capacity and throughput remain unqualified.

Pending native sharing charges its encoded File bytes under an independent
retained-memory owner until the share promise settles. Closing the export sheet
cannot remove that reservation early. The focused browser check holds sharing
open through panel disposal and verifies both retained and released states;
the reservation remains an estimate, not native-copy or process-memory telemetry.

## Required next work

The [scene materialization change](SCENE_MATERIALIZATION_VERIFICATION.md) now
loads adjusted depth sources at their reuse boundary and loads transformed
viewports before channel/alpha operations. Paired synthetic PNG/JPEG traces
preserve output bytes while avoiding repeated execution of the lazy transform
chain. Some measured Wasm heaps increase, so the admission estimates and CPU
policy remain unchanged. This is an execution optimization, not representative
throughput or physical-memory qualification.

Cross-tab job ownership, fenced claims/commits, and journal reconciliation now
have [implementation and Chromium fault-injection evidence](FOLDER_RECOVERY_VERIFICATION.md).
Physical filesystem/crash qualification remains open. Origin-wide CPU and
currently accounted memory admission are implemented above; the full retained
memory and physical lifecycle qualifications remain required.
Text composition now runs inside admitted workers with surface/font estimates,
late font copies and one Pillow final encode. Loaded editor fonts contribute
to retained memory. [Real 1/4/8-worker text checks](TEXT_COMPOSITOR_VERIFICATION.md)
produce identical output. [Scene rendering](SCENE_COMPOSITOR_VERIFICATION.md)
now uses the same admission policy with photo/mask/font/tile estimates, deferred
asset copies and frozen revisions; twelve outputs at each of 1/4/8 workers match.
Complete accounting for decoded DOM previews, font restoration peaks, storage
hashing, browser caches and future models remains open.
Speculative story and photo-look thumbnails now use this scheduler. Future
inference must also join it with explicit model/thread reservations.

The first [canonical collection matrix](COLLECTION_BENCHMARKS.md) now includes
pinned small/camera stimuli, five randomized cold/warm repetitions, actual
journaled saves, strict output checks and stage timings. It measured 1/2/4/8/Auto
on this host; 16 exceeded its CPU budget. All output checks passed, but Auto
timing passes only the warm camera family. A separate probe reproduced false
backoff across an idle gap in that measured source. The current scheduler clears
partial windows when all work drains. With that archived correction, the original
probe and six maintained regressions passed; learned limits and completed
measurements survived. The current maintained regressions retain that behavior.
New timing
evidence in run `benchmark-e00ad0b2-5afe-4761-9318-91eed9a956db` passes all
100 measured jobs. Both warm Auto families meet the 10% timing target; cold
Auto and the tiny PNG regression remain failing. The complete budget ledger is
11 pass, 6 fail and 4 unproven, with final source/raw checks passing. The dirty
worktree and the remaining release gates prevent production qualification.
The subsequent bounded-startup run on 20 September also passes all 100 measured
jobs and brings the ledger to 15 pass, 2 fail and 4 unproven: cold camera Auto
met timing too; cold small Auto and tiny PNG failed at that revision. Both exact
source revisions, complete result sets and their tested sites are archived.
The later initial-arrival calibration closes the cold-small Auto failure on this
host; its complete ledger and remaining tiny-PNG failure are recorded above.
The plan still requires the heavy scene family, Auto budgets on required devices, complete
memory/interaction evidence and sustained physical phone tests. Cross-engine
and interrupted-write tests cannot be replaced by Chromium emulation. User
research, mobile accessibility, product features, and all release gates also
remain in scope.
